======================================================================
RESULT 2  the divisibility ladder (h = t; genuine polynomial arithmetic)
======================================================================
   with a = alpha t^k and p = beta t^(k-1), divide the third-step
   expression by t^(k-2) and read which terms survive at t = 0.
   k = 4: value at t = 0 is 9*beta*qq0
            = (9*beta) * q(0)   -> forces q(0) = 0, i.e. t | q
   k = 5: value at t = 0 is 16*beta*qq0
            = (16*beta) * q(0)   -> forces q(0) = 0, i.e. t | q
   k = 6: value at t = 0 is 25*beta*qq0
            = (25*beta) * q(0)   -> forces q(0) = 0, i.e. t | q
  [PASS] at k = 4,5,6 the third step forces t | q_(k-1) (coefficient of q(0) is nonzero and nothing else survives at t = 0)
   substituting q = t*qt and dividing by t again yields a further
   condition: a divisibility ladder, as on the framework side.

======================================================================
RESULT 3  the k = 4 branch over h = t, decided at bounded degree
======================================================================
   C_4 = (0, alpha t^4),  C_3 = (beta t^3, q3), all else free
   (Jacobian constant)*alpha*beta inverted: GB = {1} means NO SOLUTION
   coefficient degree <= 1: 17 conditions, 16 unknowns -> GB = {1}, NO SOLUTION
   coefficient degree <= 2: 26 conditions, 23 unknowns -> GB = {1}, NO SOLUTION
  [PASS] no order-4 sweep over h = t at coefficient degree <= 1
  [PASS] no order-4 sweep over h = t at coefficient degree <= 2

